        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.chipware.installation.host_only", "Chipware only functions inside cyberware that exposes chip slots.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.chipware.installation.slot_count", "The active chip slot count comes from the host implant and its upgrade state.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.ripper.subtitle", "Install, compare and manage cyberware, chipware and modules.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.ripper.overview.1", "The Ripper Station is the main body-management screen.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.ripper.overview.2", "It exposes body slots, current installed hardware, chrome totals and slot-by-slot planning.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.ripper.functions.1", "Install and remove cyberware.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.ripper.functions.2", "Inspect passive effects, active implants, upgrade level and integrity.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.ripper.functions.3", "Manage chipware and modules when the selected implant supports them.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.ripper.functions.4", "Open the archive directly from the station UI.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.tech.subtitle", "Restore damaged cyberware while retaining upgrades and custom data.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.tech.overview.1", "The Repair Station restores existing implants. Upgrade levels are applied at the separate [[station_upgrade|Upgrade Station]].");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.tech.overview.2", "The Repair Station shares the [[station_upgrade|Upgrade Station]] layout and controls. Each four-second repair restores 25% of maximum integrity to the same implant. Normally, repair requires at least 25% missing integrity. With a Neural Fault, the first cycle only clears the fault and leaves integrity unchanged. Subsequent cycles repair integrity normally.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.tech.overview.3", "Place materials manually, shift-click them or use Fill materials; Creative fills are free. Stop cancels unfinished work without consuming inputs. Inventory and progress remain in the station after closing or saving. Hoppers and pipes feed implants and materials from above or the sides and retrieve completed items below. Each rising redstone edge starts one repair; busy or invalid pulses are ignored without queuing.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.tech.functions.1", "A Neural Fault makes the integrity bar fully red until cleared. Otherwise, the bar shows current integrity and a faint preview of the next 25% repair. Each cycle costs one quarter of the configured repair materials, rounded up to at least one item of each type.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.tech.functions.2", "All implant tiers are supported from the start. No machine or personal service capacity upgrades are required.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.tech.functions.3", "Repairs preserve installed chips, modules, upgrade levels and custom names. Neural Faults disable an implant and its attached chips or modules until a Repair Station clears the fault. [[cyberpsychosis|Cyberpsychosis]] has a 35% chance to leave one fault; with at least three implants, 20% of those events affect a second implant. Each loses 35-60% of maximum integrity. Normal gameplay wear is one quarter of its previous rate.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.recycler.subtitle", "Break down cyberware, modules, chipware and consumables into reusable parts.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.recycler.overview.1", "The Recycler previews recovery from server recipes. Press Recycle to start a three-second cycle per input. It repeats until input empties or the 5 by 5 recovery grid fills. Stop cancels an unfinished cycle without consuming input.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.recycler.overview.2", "Items and progress stay in the station when its screen closes. Hoppers and pipes insert from above or the sides and extract recovery from below. Each rising redstone edge recycles exactly one input. The signal may turn off while it runs. Pulses while busy or unable to recycle are ignored, never queued; a held signal does not repeat.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.recycler.functions.1", "Recycle cyberware into up to three function-specific material types. Good, worn and heavily damaged implants have distinct, deterministic recovery bands.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.recycler.functions.2", "Recycle limb modules into micro actuators.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.recycler.functions.3", "Recycle chipware into firmware modules.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.recycler.functions.4", "Recycle consumables into wiring, paste or circuit plates by category.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.title", "Relic Caches");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.subtitle", "Tiered breach caches placed into high-value structures.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.overview.1", "Each placed Relic Cache keeps its own matrix and daemon list until that cache is opened.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.overview.2", "Plan before selecting: the first pick starts the breach timer. Uncommon/Rare/Epic/Legendary caches allow 50/40/32/25 seconds. Closing an executing breach does not pause it.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.overview.3", "Caches can award intact equipment, service materials, schematic discoveries and specialized fabrication parts. Item rewards depend on the server loot tables. Successfully breaching an Uncommon/Rare/Epic/Legendary cache also awards 20/35/55/80 XP points once per player and cache. Failed attempts give no XP. Survival caches cannot be collected and relocated for repeated rewards; claim history survives death.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.spawn_sites.1", "Uncommon caches replace one chest or barrel in igloo basements, jungle temples and pillager outposts.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.spawn_sites.2", "Rare caches replace one chest or barrel in desert pyramids, woodland mansions and strongholds.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.spawn_sites.3", "Epic caches replace one chest or barrel in trial chambers, ancient cities and bastion remnants.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.spawn_sites.4", "Legendary caches replace one chest or barrel in end cities.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.spawn_sites.5", "Bundled loot includes one primary reward, one or two support rolls and one fabrication-discovery roll. Server datapacks can change these pools.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.spawn_sites.6", "Placement also checks older loaded structures, but only untouched loot-table chests or barrels. Opened containers, player storage and double chests are preserved. Each supported structure receives at most one generated cache.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.spawn_sites.7", "Ask a [[npc_fixer|Fixer]] or [[npc_netrunner|Netrunner]] about nearby relic caches to mark the nearest known cache you have not breached on your HUD compass. No compass item is required. They do not reveal unexplored terrain. The Relic Scanner highlights every cache tier and prioritizes caches over ordinary containers within scan range.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.functions.1", "Select tokens from the highlighted top row first, then alternate between the matching column and matching row.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.functions.2", "A daemon is cleared once its full token sequence appears anywhere in the path you have built.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.functions.3", "Clear every daemon before time or buffer runs out. Buffers hold 7/8/8/9 picks for Uncommon/Rare/Epic/Legendary caches. Higher tiers require longer overlapping sequences.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.functions.4", "Daemons do not grant separate effects or partial rewards. The cache opens only when every listed daemon is cleared.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.example.grid_label", "Example Route");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.example.daemon_label", "Daemon Chain");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.example.1", "This is a simplified example of overlapping sequences, not a complete high-tier puzzle. Start at top-row step 1 for 55.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.example.2", "Your second pick must stay in the same column, so move down to step 2 for BD, then across that row to step 3 for 4C.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.example.3", "At that point Daemon 1 is already cleared, because 55 BD 4C now appears in your path.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.example.4", "From step 3, move down the same column to FF, then across the last row to 0A. Those last two picks clear Daemon 2 as BD 4C FF and Daemon 3 as 4C FF 0A.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.how_to_manage.1", "Start by scanning the daemon sequences on the right and look for shared middle or ending tokens.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.how_to_manage.2", "Because the path only has to contain the sequence, you can route one daemon into another instead of solving each one separately.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.how_to_manage.3", "Selected cells are numbered on the grid, so you can review the full route directly while planning the next pick.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.how_to_manage.4", "Hovered cells show whether the current line is legal. If a token is off the active row or column, it cannot be picked yet.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.station.relic_cache.how_to_manage.5", "You can close the screen to deal with combat or reposition, then reopen the same cache and continue the same run if time remains.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.section.example_solution", "Example Solution");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general", "General");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.subtitle", "Shared NPC rules, contact tracking and identity behavior across the whole NPC layer.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.overview.1", "NPCs retain their identity, home and per-player relationships.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.city_navigation", "On your first login, the nearest Night City is marked on your HUD compass to help you meet NPCs. Entering that city disables its marker once, without deleting it. Open Neural Archive > Compass > Markers and choose Cities to show it again, rename it or change its color. The location, visit status and visibility are saved for your player across deaths and reconnects.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.overview.2", "Each player keeps separate trust and interaction flags with the same NPC. Blue names mean friendly; hurting a contact turns their name yellow for you for five minutes. They turn red and defend themselves below 25% health. An amber bar below their health shows the remaining aggression cooldown and refills when you hit them again. After five seconds without another attack, or when you leave the immediate area, they stop fighting and return to yellow. Anger fades after five minutes; temporary refusals last at most four minutes after the last provocation. Trust can be earned back. Surviving contacts start healing after 20 seconds without damage, recovering one health point every five seconds. Red names mean aggressive enemies, including cyberpsychos and MaxTec. Name colors indicate behavior and cannot be customized. Killing a contact deploys a four-officer MaxTec squad against the killer, with dedicated skins and working combat cyberware. Sandevistan slows cyberpsychos and MaxTec even when they are idle or targeting someone else. Player-credited kills drop XP: 2 for ordinary NPCs, 25 per MaxTec officer, and 50/75/100 for tier 1/2/3 cyberpsychos. Combat XP follows vanilla mob-loot rules.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.overview.3", "NPCs use player-style skins and rendering, can be called through the archive, and are meant to stay relevant beyond a single screen click.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.contacts_archive.1", "The Contacts tab records every NPC you have actually interacted with. Each NPC card shows the head render, name, type, trust state and live or last known location. Online players also appear as Player contacts, with their skin, voice availability and Pin, Block and Call controls. Use the type filter or search to find them. Pinned and blocked player contacts remain listed offline. Blocking prevents calls in either direction; use Unblock on their card to undo it.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.contacts_archive.2", "Search contacts by name or NPC type, then use Type: All types to select an archetype. All, Pinned, Deceased and Hidden filters work together with search and type selection. Hidden contacts appear only under Hidden.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.contacts_archive.3", "Each card supports Pin or Unpin plus Hide or Unhide. These flags are stored per player, so one player's organization does not change another player's archive.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.contacts_archive.4", "Press K during gameplay for quick-call contacts with faces and colored names. Pinned contacts appear first. Use Alt+1 through Alt+3 to call a visible contact, Alt+4 for the next page and Alt+5 to close. Calls use the same compact HUD while you keep moving and playing: Alt+1 through Alt+5 answer, Alt+0 hangs up. All controls can be rebound in Minecraft Controls.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.identity_config.1", "NPC names and categories come from config/cyberneticenhancements-npc-identities.properties.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.identity_config.2", "The file separates male_names, female_names and generic_names. The chosen category also selects the texture folder under assets/cyberneticenhancements/textures/entity/npc/<category>/.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.identity_config.3", "Skins are rendered on the player model with second-layer parts enabled, so hats, jackets and similar overlay pixels show if the texture provides them.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.current_state.1", "Five archetypes are live: [[npc_fixer|Fixer]], [[npc_ripperdoc|Ripperdoc]], [[npc_techie|Techie]], [[npc_netrunner|Netrunner]] and [[npc_merc|Merc]]. Each has its own stock, services and dialogue.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.general.current_state.2", "Techies, Netrunners and Mercs issue [[quest_guide_overview|contracts]], including Cyberpsycho Sightings. Fixers and Netrunners offer cache intel.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.fixer.trust_model.1", "A business introduction marks the contact as known and gives a small opening trust gain.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.fixer.trust_model.2", "Successful business and completed contracts build a gradual relationship. The contact screen shows your trust percentage and next unlock.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.fixer.trust_model.3", "Trust is stored per player and per contact UUID. Rapport with one contact does not unlock another.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.fixer.trust_model.4", "Attacking a contact, wasting a requested meetup, or repeatedly attempting unaffordable purchases can lower trust.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.fixer.trust_model.5", "Accumulated annoyance can temporarily increase prices or make the contact refuse service.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.npc.fixer.trust_model.6", "Heat can cause a roughly two-minute price surcharge or four-minute refusal window. Let it expire before returning.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.cyberstrain.subtitle", "The pressure system that limits how much chrome a body can run safely.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.cyberstrain.threshold.psychosis", "Psychosis Risk: %s");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.cyberstrain.change.1", "Cyberstrain has no passive stat penalties. At or above the psychosis-risk threshold, accumulated instability can trigger an [[cyberpsychosis|episode]].");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.cyberstrain.change.2", "Suppression medicine temporarily reduces effective cyberstrain.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.cyberstrain.change.3", "Frontal cortex hardware can add extra cyberstrain on top of installed chrome.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.cyberstrain.manage.1", "Use lower-cost implants for early builds.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.cyberstrain.manage.2", "Use suppressants when pushing into high-strain setups.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.cyberstrain.manage.3", "Treat active cyberware and stimulant use as part of the risk budget, not just the installed load.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.psychosis.subtitle", "High-strain crisis behavior driven by effective cyberstrain plus instability.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.psychosis.trigger.1", "Cyberpsychosis does not trigger from installed chrome alone. It requires psychosis-risk [[cyberstrain|cyberstrain]] plus accumulated instability.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.psychosis.trigger.2", "Instability builds from combat, cyberware activations, certain consumables and remaining at or above the psychosis-risk threshold.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.psychosis.tiers.1", "Minor episode: temporary combat bonuses and involuntary pursuit, preceded by an onset animation.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.psychosis.tiers.2", "Major episode: stronger combat bonuses and involuntary pursuit. Both episode types end with an exhausted recovery animation.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.psychosis.stabilization.1", "Immunoblockers and Chrome Suppressant reduce instability and can shorten an active episode.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.psychosis.stabilization.2", "After an episode ends, Aftershock remains briefly and keeps some penalties active.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.psychosis.targeting.1", "Episodes prioritize reachable players, NPCs, pets, hostile mobs, then passive mobs. Stalled targets are temporarily skipped.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.psychosis.targeting.2", "Ignored targets include configured nuisance types such as bats, fish, silverfish and endermites.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.progression.subtitle", "The live acquisition split and implemented world sources.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.progression.craftable.1", "Cyberware, chipware, limb modules and advanced parts are manufactured in the [[station_fabricator|Fabricator]]. Basic supplies, consumables and machine blocks also use crafting-table recipes.");
        add("wiki.cyberneticenhancements.system.progression.craftable.2", "Public designs are available immediately. Schematic-locked recipes remain hidden and unusable until learned, including in JEI and the Fabricator.");
